## The plug-in's two loading differences, as switches, 2026-11-26.

# The row this probe opened named the differences it could not yet test: the
# plug-in loads a private copy of the core as onnxruntime-ofx.dll and resolves the
# api from that module, and it loads the CUDA groups before it registers the
# provider. Both are now switches - --private-core and --preload - and the
# question is no longer what the api does, but whether either of those turns the
# console sequence into the plug-in's abort.
#
# Provider, model and machine as above: onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll from the
# 1.32.0 GPU package, .validation/big_conv.onnx (1x4x256x256 -> 262144 values),
# Windows 11, MSVC 14.50. The private core is a byte copy of that package's
# onnxruntime.dll under the plug-in's name.
#
# Commands:
#   ep-register-probe big_conv.onnx <bin>/onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll
#   ep-register-probe ... --private-core <bin>/onnxruntime-ofx.dll
#   ep-register-probe ... --preload <bin>/cudart64_13.dll --preload <bin>/cublas64_13.dll \
#       --preload <bin>/cublasLt64_13.dll --preload <bin>/cufft64_12.dll --preload <bin>/cudnn64_9.dll
#   both together, then --stop 2, --stop 3 and --stop 6 with both on

